// The sprites of Dead Revolver's pixel UI pack, by name.
//
// The art is in the Aseprite files of the pack as they come, nothing was exported from them. Every file holds the same art
// once for every colour it comes in, a layer (or a group of layers) to a colour, and what a sheet file has to say about
// an image that is an Aseprite file is mostly which layer and which frame:
//   themes:   the layer every theme is drawn on, whatever layer the file was saved showing
//   fallback: the theme that is used when a sprite is asked for in a theme the image doesn't come in
//             (the first one listed if it doesn't say)
//   states:   the frame of the file the other states of every sprite are on, by its number or by the name of its tag:
//             with states: { hover: 1 } every sprite of the image is there a second time as "name_hover"
//   hide:     layers that are never drawn. Most of these files have a Background layer that is only there to draw against
//   size:     how big the sprites of the image are, for images that are a grid of them: a number or
//             vec(width, height). A sprite can still say otherwise with a w and an h of its own
// A sprite says where it is on the canvas of the file, in pixels from the top left:
//   x, y, w, h: the rectangle of the sprite (of its first frame, if it has several)
//   slice:      the name of a slice of the file to be the rectangle instead, which brings its nine-slice border along
//   tag:        the name of a tag of the file, whose frames are the frames of the sprite: played the way the tag says
//               and for as long as the file says every frame takes. skip: 2 leaves the first two of them out
//   frame:      the frame of the file it is on (by its number or the name of a tag), for one that isn't an animation
//   layer:      the one layer it is drawn on whatever the theme, for files whose layers aren't colours of the same thing
//   crop:       true shrinks the rectangle to what is actually drawn in it over all of its frames
//   border:     how much of every edge keeps its size when the sprite is stretched as a nine-slice: a number for
//               all four sides, vec(horizontal, vertical) or vec(left, top, right, bottom)
//   content:    how far in from every edge whatever is put on top of the sprite goes, written like a border: the
//               label of a button sits on its face, not on the lip under it
//   frames:     how many frames it has side by side on the canvas ("name#2" is the third on its own), or a list of
//               where every frame after the first is, vec(x, y) each, for frames scattered about the image
//   step:       how far the next frame is from the one before, for frames that aren't simply side by side: vec(right, down)
//   time:       how long every frame of an animation is shown for, in seconds
//   cell:       where it is as a cell of the grid of its image, vec(column, row), instead of x and y
// An image can have a grid: cell: 16 (or vec(w, h)) is how big a cell is, origin: vec(x, y) where the grid starts, and
// then its sprites can be written by their cell.
// A sprite can also be asked for in a theme of its own, whatever the skin's theme is: "round_blank@white".
//
// An image can still be a plain picture (the gamepads and the keyboard at the bottom are). Then the art of every theme
// is side by side in it, see SpriteSheetDefinition for how that is written down (block, and themes and states as numbers).
//
// This file is a program. Anything written down over and over is better written as a loop, see the keyboard below.
// Only sprites that are used get loaded, so there is no harm in naming more of the pack here than a UI needs.
// The rectangles were measured off the art, see the files themselves (in Aseprite) for what else there is to name.

